ertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92e55227-bf1c-5ea6-85e1-7b51ef62ef62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92e55227-bf1c-5ea6-85e1-7b51ef62ef62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293508} :answer-field/id #uuid "440e9a61-755c-5fee-8379-dccea5769774" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294171} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1196f9d-7e68-5b43-b2c7-5648f5cf9901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1196f9d-7e68-5b43-b2c7-5648f5cf9901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293622} :answer-field/id #uuid "88343c54-e5b4-5c7e-8b0e-7c3041c31c26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d82ddc72-1ed9-51d4-9209-e788e94b9b5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-279197" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=12$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=12,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447870c5-0e16-591a-8300-97b1a1b53090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333622} {:answer/id #uuid "548b03ca-4238-5b00-9cb4-91def2cc07cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\sqrt{2}$ and $4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333761} {:answer/id #uuid "68381630-1c58-5c47-b731-887a1f1decb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333945} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa132ec-2b5e-506f-9009-d06c05f62c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333966} {:answer/id #uuid "f94f55ce-3720-511d-baaa-529e5639ba46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7\\sqrt{2}$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "447870c5-0e16-591a-8300-97b1a1b53090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333622} :answer-field/id #uuid "c27addb0-e02b-555b-8abb-615b89631ce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50aac069-cc6f-51ae-b729-84e72b0989dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-69563" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e7bd1b3-9829-5473-8614-8c430afd35b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343033} {:answer/id #uuid "6886ac93-669b-567a-bcd2-cdc962ddc459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343131} {:answer/id #uuid "84a8edc1-a88f-5434-afb0-143e81012a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 175921863433